## 3 Neutron star properties.

The moment of inertia and tidal deformability of a neutron star are calculated by numerically integrating a system of equations of stellar structure. The Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff equations (Tolman, 1939; Oppenheimer & Volkoff, 1939)

\\frac{dp}{dr}=-\\frac{G(\\mu+p)(m+4\\pi r^{3}p/c^{2})}{c^{2}r^{2}f},\\qquad\\frac{dm}{ dr}=4\\pi r^{2}\\mu/c^{2},  where f(r):=1-2Gm/c^{2}r, determine the profiles of total energy density \\mu(r), pressure p(r) and mass m(r) throughout the star. The stellar radius R is defined by the condition p(R)=0, and the star's mass is M:=m(R). The moment of inertia is computed by solving Hartle's slow rotation equation (Hartle, 1967)

0= \\,rf\\frac{d^{2}\\omega}{dr^{2}}+4\\left[f-\\pi r^{2}(\\mu/c^{2}+p/c^{2 })\\right]\\frac{d\\omega}{dr} -16\\pi r(\\mu/c^{2}+p/c^{2})\\omega  for the frame-dragging function \\omega(r); its surficial value \\omega_{s}:=\\omega(R) fixes the moment of inertia via I=(1-\\omega_{s})c^{2}R^{3}/2G. The tidal perturbation \\eta(r) of the star's spacetime metric is governed by the equation (Landry & Poisson, 2014)

r\\frac{d\\eta}{dr}+\\eta(\\eta-1)+A\\eta-B=0,  where

A =2f^{-1}\\left[1-\\frac{3Gm}{c^{2}r}-2\\pi r^{2}(\\mu/c^{2}+3p/c^{2 })\\right],  B =f^{-1}\\left[6-4\\pi r^{2}(\\mu/c^{2}+p/c^{2})\\left(3+\\frac{d\\mu}{ dp}\\right)\\right]. 

The tidal deformability is related to the surficial value \\eta_{s}:=\\eta(R) of the pertubation through

\\Lambda= \\frac{\\eta_{s}-2-4C/f_{s}}{3C^{5}\\left[R\\,dF/dr-(\\eta_{s}+3-4C/f_{s} )\\,F\\right]},  where C:=GM/c^{2}R, f_{s}:=f(R), and F(r):={}_{2}F_{1}(3,5,6,2GM/c^{2}r) is a hypergeometric function; F and dF/dr are evaluated at r=R.

With a specification of the equation of state to close the system, Eqs. (1)-(3) are integrated simultaneously from the center of the star, where the central density \\rho_{c}:=\\rho(0) must be prescribed, to its surface. A sequence of stable neutron stars is constructed by sampling \\rho_{c} values up to \\rho_{\\rm max}, the central density for which the stellar mass reaches a maximum M_{\\rm max}. Beyond \\rho_{\\rm max}, the stars become unstable to radial perturbations (Harrison et al., 1965).

Selecting 50 logarithmically spaced central densities in the interval \\left[1.0,8.5\\right]\\rho_{\\rm nuc} for the integrations, we compute a stable mass-sequence for each of our 53 equations of state. The moment of inertia and tidal deformability data obtained in this way are displayed in Figs. 1 and 2. The dimensionless moment of inertia \\bar{I}(M) plotted in Fig. 1 is a decreasing function of the mass, with stiffer equations of state producing larger \\bar{I} at fixed M. Because Eq. (2) neglects rotational and tidal deformations of the neutron star, the moment of inertia we compute is that of a spherical star. The corrections to I enter at second order in the dimensionless spin \\chi:=cS/GM^{2} and at first order in the tidal perturbation \\epsilon:=\\delta R/R. Since neutrons stars in binaries are expected to rotate slowly,3 spin corrections to the stellar structure are of order \\chi^{2}\\sim 10^{-3}. Similarly, tidal corrections to the neutron star's shape are of order (R/r_{12})^{3}\\sim 10^{-15}, where r_{12} is the binary separation.4 We therefore neglect both O(\\chi^{2}) and O(\\epsilon) corrections to I in this work.

