## 4 Universal Relations

The binary Love and I-Love relations are calculated by performing log-log polynomial fits to the tidal deformability and moment of inertia data computed for the equations of state of interest. For the binary Love relation, the tidal deformabilities \\Lambda_{1.4}:=\\Lambda(1.4\\,M_{\\odot}) and \\Lambda_{\\star}:=\\Lambda(1.338\\,M_{\\odot}) for each model are plotted against one another in Fig. 3. A fit to the relation

\\log_{10}\\Lambda_{\\star}=\\sum_{n=0}^{1}a_{n}(\\log_{10}\\Lambda_{1.4})^{n}  is performed, with the coefficients a_{n} determined by least-squares regression (see Table 1). We have chosen a linear fit for the relation so as not to bias the extrapolation to the sparsely populated low-\\Lambda region of the plot, where the lower bound on the tidal deformability is located. Our fit is consistent with the \\Lambda_{\\star}-\\Lambda_{1.4} relation implied by the approximate scaling \\Lambda(M)\\propto(R/M)^{6} identified in De et al. (2018). As expected, the tidal deformabilities for all the equations of state new closely to the fit, with deviations \\Delta\\Lambda_{\\star}=|\\Lambda_{\\star}-\\Lambda_{\\star}^{\\rm fit}|/\\Lambda_{ \\star}^{\\rm fit} of no more than 3%. The fit residuals are plotted in the lower panel of Fig. 3.

The I-Love relation is calculated in a similar fashion. For each equation of state in our set, the moment of inertia and tidal deformability mass-sequences (for M\\in[M_{\\odot},M_{\\rm max}]) are plotted against one another, as shown in Fig. 4, and a fit to the relation

\\log_{10}\\bar{I}=\\sum_{n=0}^{4}c_{n}(\\log_{10}\\Lambda)^{n}  is performed, yielding the coefficients c_{n} listed in Table 1. The deviations from the fit are also plotted in Fig. 4, and do not exceed 0.6% error. Our fit is nearly identical to the original I-Love relation calculated in Yagi and Yunes (2013), though our residuals are slightly smaller because we omit the unrealistic polytropic models included there. We consider our recomputed fit to be more reliable than the original one because it is based on a larger, more representative set of equations of state.

Taken together, the binary Love and I-Love relations imply that

\\log_{10}\\bar{I}_{\\star}=\\sum_{n=0}^{4}c_{n}(a_{0}+a_{1}\\log_{10}\\Lambda_{1.4} )^{n}. 

Applying this formula to the 90%-credible \\Lambda_{1.4} constraints from GW170817, we obtain the bounds \\bar{I}=11.10^{+3.64}_{-2.28}. (The upper bound derived from the initial analysis of GW170817 is \\bar{I}\\leq 16.08.) These correspond to the constraints on I_{\\star} given in the Intro

